cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  conjprob: collision probability for a close approach with the validity regime
  stated next to the number
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-21
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Collision probability for a close approach, computed by several independent
  routes, with the disagreement between them reported as the primary output
  rather than as a footnote. Three analytic paths are implemented separately
  over the same encounter-plane integral -- the Foster-Estes short-encounter
  formulation evaluated by adaptive quadrature of the error-function reduction,
  Chan's equivalent-area series, and Alfano's fixed-node Simpson evaluation --
  and are scored against two references that share no algebra with them: an
  independent two-dimensional polar quadrature, and a three-dimensional Monte
  Carlo over the relative state with exact Clopper-Pearson intervals. On a
  circular encounter-plane covariance the exact probability is the non-central
  chi-square distribution function, and Foster-Estes, Chan at its recommended
  series order and the quadrature reference agree with scipy.stats.ncx2 to
  6.661e-16, 1.110e-15 and 5.551e-16 worst relative error respectively, while
  Alfano's fixed-node rule sits at 1.479e-04 with a measured convergence order of
  1.5004 set by the square-root boundary singularity of the chord. The validity
  regimes are reported as measured curves against the quadrature reference: at a
  one per cent tolerance Chan's equivalent-area substitution is exact at
  covariance aspect ratio one and its boundary falls from R / sigma_eff = 0.5609
  at aspect 2 to 0.02024 at aspect 30, and the leading-order small-hard-body
  approximation has a boundary from 0.2818 to 0.01958 over the same grid, with
  worst errors anywhere on the grid of 2.906285e+01 and 3.933262e+00. The dilution
  region is exhibited explicitly as a Pc-against-covariance-scale curve with its
  non-monotonic turn located, the closed-form maximum R^2 / (e d^2) reproduced to
  4.441e-16, and a DilutionWarning raised by the library itself; the shipped
  low-Earth case reports a probability 2.0064 times smaller than the maximum
  attainable over covariance scale. Monte Carlo interval coverage is measured at
  0.960 over 300 independent seeds against a nominal 0.95, with an interval-width
  exponent of -0.5052. The rectilinear-relative-motion assumption is violated
  deliberately: the two-dimensional formulation reaches one per cent error at a
  measured curvature parameter of 3.288 and ten per cent at 670.3, which for a
  declared low Earth encounter corresponds to relative speeds of 0.810 and 0.057
  metres per second, so the formulation is excellent for a fast conjunction and
  unusable for a slow one. A learned random-forest screen over cheap geometry and
  covariance features is benchmarked against three analytic screens on recall at
  a fixed false-positive rate. The result is reported as measured and is mixed:
  at a one per cent false-positive rate the learned model reaches 0.99284 recall
  on its training regime against 0.97733 for a one-line closed-form screen using
  the same features, and loses on both regimes it was not trained on, 0.98840
  against 1.00000 and 0.99784 against 1.00000. The specification's prediction
  that a Mahalanobis threshold would match the learned model does not hold on the
  broad population, where that screen is the worst of the three at 0.03436; it
  recovers to 0.83720 on a population in which R / sigma_eff varies by less than
  a factor of two, so the prediction holds under a condition that is identified
  and measured here. Operational conjunction assessment is done with CARA- and
  CSpOC-grade tooling against catalogues this product does not have; the
  contribution here is method cross-validation and regime reporting on a
  conjunction the user already has. Validation level 3, research grade. Not
  flight-qualified, not certified, not approved for operational aerospace use. No
  output of this package is suitable as an input to a manoeuvre decision.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - collision-probability
  - conjunction-assessment
  - space-situational-awareness
  - probability-dilution
  - foster-estes
  - chan-series
  - alfano-method
  - monte-carlo
  - aerospace-gnc
references:
  - type: report
    authors:
      - family-names: Foster
        given-names: "J. L."
      - family-names: Estes
        given-names: "H. S."
    title: >-
      A parametric analysis of orbital debris collision probability and maneuver
      rate for space vehicles
    institution:
      name: "NASA Johnson Space Center"
    number: "NASA/JSC-25898"
    year: 1992
    notes: >-
      The short-encounter formulation and the reduction of the encounter-plane
      area integral to one dimension with an error function in the second
      coordinate. Implemented in conjprob.analytic.pc_foster.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Chan
        given-names: "F. K."
    title: "Spacecraft Collision Probability"
    publisher:
      name: "The Aerospace Press"
    year: 2008
    notes: >-
      The equivalent-area series implemented in conjprob.analytic.pc_chan, and
      the earlier statement of it in Chan, F. K. (1997), Collision probability
      analyses for Earth-orbiting satellites, Advances in the Astronautical
      Sciences 96, 1033-1048.
